{"id":"board-speed-vendors","title":"Whose files pass fastest and slowest","question":"Among organizations with three or more files at the Board, whose pass faster or slower than like files of the same kind, size and year?","finding":"68 City-paid organizations are named in three or more timed contract, grant, lease or loan files. For 31 of them the typical gap against like files is under a week either way. 10 ran a week or more faster with no file slower than its peers (Aramark Correctional Services LLC, -14 days over 3 files; Prologis L.P., -14 days over 3 files; SF New Deal, -14 days over 3 files; Chinese Hospital Association, -9 days over 4 files), and 5 a week or more slower with none faster (Self-Help For The Elderly, +21 days over 3; San Francisco Market Corporation, +14 days over 3; Cdm Smith Inc, +7 days over 3; Alliant Insurance Services Inc, +7 days over 3). 3 of the 68 lie outside the range that random sets of the same number of files fall in 95% of the time (Urban Alchemy, slower; Japanese Community Youth Council, slower; Cross Country Staffing, slower), which is about what chance alone gives (3 of 68). No organization’s files stand out as fast-tracked.","how":"The organization is the counterparty read from the file’s title, kept only when it is a payee with at least $100,000 of outside spending in the payment file. Settlements and grants the City accepts are left out. Each file’s days to the final vote are compared with like files (same kind, type of legislation, size band and year of introduction) and the middle difference taken per organization. The chance column marks an organization whose middle difference lies outside the range that 1,000 random sets of the same number of timed files fall in 95% of the time. The table includes every qualifying organization.","limits":"Three files are few, and each can move a week or two with the meeting calendar, a break in the meetings or a continuance. A fast file is not a favoured file: complete paperwork and an uncontested renewal both pass quickly. The name is text-mined from the title and matched to the payment file by name. What would settle a row: the committee minutes for each file. The paid column counts payments issued in fiscal 2019 to 2026, which is read from the voucher number and not from the year the file lists a line under; $992.6 million of outside spending that the file lists under those years was issued earlier and is left out. Amounts, parties and dates taken from Board file titles are read by machine: in a hand check of 50 titles, 11 had at least one field read wrongly (4 after later fixes).","tier":"CLEARED","columns":[{"k":"organization","t":"Organization","type":"org"},{"k":"files","t":"Files timed","type":"int"},{"k":"median","t":"Typical days to the final vote","type":"days"},{"k":"against","t":"Against like files","type":"days"},{"k":"faster","t":"Files faster","type":"int"},{"k":"slower","t":"Files slower","type":"int"},{"k":"beyond","t":"Outside the chance range","type":"text"},{"k":"retroactive","t":"Approved after the fact","type":"int"},{"k":"value","t":"Amounts stated in the titles","type":"usd"},{"k":"paid","t":"City paid it, fiscal 2019–2026","type":"usd"},{"k":"sponsor","t":"Most frequent sponsor","type":"text"},{"k":"signs","t":"Warning signs elsewhere","type":"int"},{"k":"sign","t":"First warning sign","type":"text"},{"k":"link","t":"Latest file","type":"link"}],"rows":[{"files":3,"median":28,"against":-14,"faster":3,"slower":0,"retroactive":0,"beyond":"","value":66783723,"paid":22032609,"sponsor":"none named","signs":4,"sign":"Lead: Not in the case file itself: 2 links from a person or organization named in the file of the charged case “SF SAFE: grant money withheld from partners, and $162 gift","link":{"t":"Board file 251026","u":"https://sfgov.legistar.com/LegislationDetail.aspx?ID=7699854&GUID=C76943BE-5A5E-472B-8D97-1006D813DBB6"},"organization":"Aramark Correctional Services LLC","organizationKey":null},{"files":3,"median":28,"aga